+ "idea_html": "<p>Decide bits from 30 down to 0. Keep <code>ans</code>; try <code>cand = ans | (1&lt;&lt;b)</code>. For each x compute the cheapest <code>y ≥ x</code> containing <code>cand</code>’s bits (cost 0 if already present; otherwise h is the highest missing bit: clear x’s bits ≤ h, set h, copy <code>cand</code>’s lower bits). Sort costs; keep the bit if the m smallest sum to ≤ k. A set bit beats all lower bits combined; m elements containing <code>cand</code> have AND ⊇ <code>cand</code>, so retained bits give the maximum.</p><p>Example 1: nums = [3,1,2], k = 8, m = 2. Any candidate ≥ 8 costs 5, 7, 6; cheapest sum 5+6 = 11 &gt; 8, so bits 30..3 stay 0. Bit 2, cand = 4: costs 1, 3, 2 (3→4, 1→4, 2→4); 1+2 = 3 ≤ 8, ans = 4. Bit 1, cand = 6: 3→6 costs 3, 1→6 costs 5, 2→6 costs 4; 3+4 = 7 ≤ 8, ans = 6. Bit 0, cand = 7: 3→7 = 4, 1→7 = 6, 2→7 = 5; 4+5 = 9 &gt; 8, rejected. Answer 6, from [6, 6] at cost 7.</p><p>31 rounds of O(n) cost computation and sorting: O(31 · n log n) ≈ 31 · 5·10<sup>4</sup> · 16 ≈ 2.5·10<sup>7</sup> steps; O(n) extra space.</p>",
  "pitfalls_html": "<ul><li><strong>Binary search on the answer with <code>hi = max(nums) + k</code>.</strong> feasible(T) is not monotone in T: containing the bits of 2 does not imply containing the bits of 1. On <code>nums = [2], k = 0, m = 1</code> the first probe is T = 1, which costs 1 &gt; 0, so <code>hi</code> drops to 0 and the search returns 0; the answer is 2. Three recorded Wrong Answers (661–667 of 1032) do this. The same search with <code>lo = 0, hi = 2<sup>31</sup></code> probes exactly <code>ans | (1&lt;&lt;b)</code> in order (checked on 2000 random inputs) and is the bit-by-bit method in disguise; two accepted files rely on that.</li><li><strong>Cost = <code>(target | (x &amp; ~target)) − x</code>, i.e. keep x's extra bits and set the missing ones.</strong> A carry can be cheaper. On <code>nums = [1, 5], k = 2, m = 2</code>, target 2: 5 needs only 6 (cost 1) but the formula gives 7 (cost 2), total 3 &gt; 2, so it returns 1; the answer is 2 from [2, 6]. Recorded Wrong Answer, 637/1032.</li><li><strong>Take the m largest values and optimize only those.</strong> Alignment beats size. On <code>[5, 3, 3], k = 0, m = 2</code> the top two give 5 &amp; 3 = 1, the two 3s give 3. On <code>[12, 5, 5], k = 2, m = 2</code> the top two reach at most 4; the two 5s become [6, 6] for 6.</li><li><strong>Fixing the <em>lowest</em> missing bit first, or stopping at bit 29.</strong> Each low-bit fix clears the bits below it and un-fixes them again: for x = 1 and mask 2<sup>b</sup>−1 the loop runs 2<sup>b</sup>−2 times (measured 16.7M iterations at b = 24). <code>[1073741823, 536870911], k = 0, m = 1</code> makes the recorded C++ TLE take about 14 s locally against 0.4 s for the highest-bit-first versions. Separately, values can reach 2·10<sup>9</sup> &gt; 2<sup>30</sup>: on <code>[10<sup>9</sup>], k = 73741824, m = 1</code> the answer is 2<sup>30</sup> = 1073741824, and a loop over bits 29..0 returns 1073741823 (recorded Wrong Answer, 1030/1032).</li></ul>",
