de`, `go build`).'"\\n\\nResults, 302/302 each: python3 kimi-k2.5-cloud-t0.0, glm-5-cloud, deepseek-v4-flash-cloud-t0.0; java codex-agent-gpt-5.5-java, kimi-k2.5-cloud-t0.0-java; cpp kimi-k2.5-cloud-t0.0-cpp; javascript glm-5-cloud-t0.8-javascript, kimi-k2.5-cloud-t0.0-javascript; golang kimi-k2.5-cloud-t0.0-golang, minimax-m2.7-cloud-t1.0-golang. claude-sonnet-4-6: 114/302 (all failures are cancel-before-add). qwen3.5-cloud: 0/302 (NameError). Shipped code (comments added, JS boilerplate comment removed) re-run: 302/302 in all five languages.\\n\\n## Unsettled\\n\\nRandom sequences agreeing with my reference are not LeetCode's 844 tests; the LeetCode Accepted verdicts stand behind the shipped files.\\n\",\"before\":332},{\"path\":\"review/rungs/wc0483-Q4-minimum-cost-to-merge-sorted-lists.md\",\"text\":\"# wc0483-Q4 Minimum Cost to Merge Sorted Lists — review note (2026-09-08)\\n\\n## Original text\\n\\n- Tempting 1, its own example [1],[2],[1000]: \\\"final cost 3+(2+1+998)=1004\\\". median([1,2]) = 1, so the second merge costs 2+1+999 = 1002 and greedy totals 1005. \\\"[1]+[2,1000] (cost=1+2+|1−500|=502)\\\": median([2,1000]) is the left middle, 2; cost 4; that order totals 1000+4 = 1004. So \\\"greedy wins here\\\" is false: the example is itself a counterexample (greedy 1005, optimal 1004; brute force over all merge orders agrees). No other failing input was given.\\n- Tempting 2: no failing input. Found [[6],[3],[6]]: interval DP 11, optimal 8.\\n- Tempting 3: \\\"the 2 TLE submissions likely fell into\\\" per-transition median recomputation. Record: 5 TLE + 1 WA. All five TLE files precompute medians (two deepseek Python files via heapq.merge per subset; claude-haiku java re-merges lists on every improving split; two qwen files use a tuple-of-lists state DP). The WA (codex-low java, 586/881) stacks two symmetry filters and skips splits; fails on [[5],[1],[3]] (11 vs 9).\\n- Essence: \\\"531 441 submask transitions\\\" is 3"'^12; exact pair count 3''^12 − 2''^13 + 1 = 523 250.'"\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\nScratch dir: "'`experiments.py` (brute force over merge orders, bitmask reference, greedy, interval DP, the codex-low filter; 4000 random n ≤ 5 inputs, bitmask == brute on all; minimal counterexamples by random search), `harness.py` (4 LeetCode testcases + 36 random, n up to 12 and 2000 elements, expected from the reference; per-language drivers), `timing.py` (one n = 12, 2000-element input).'"\\n\\n40/40: python3 claude-sonnet-4-6, kimi-k2.5-cloud-t0.0, deepseek-v4-pro-cloud-t1.0, and both deepseek TLE files (slow, not wrong); java kimi-k2.5-cloud-t0.0-java, qwen3.5-cloud-t1.0-java; cpp kimi; javascript kimi, deepseek-v4-pro; golang minimax-m2.7-cloud-t0.0-golang, kimi. codex-low java: 16/40. Timing on the max input: sonnet py 0.14 s, deepseek heapq versions 0.43/0.59 s, kimi java 0.07 s, haiku java (with the missing "'`import java.util.*` added) 0.27 s. Shipped code with comments re-run: 40/40 in all five languages.'"\\n\\n## Unsettled\\n\\nLeetCode's runtime figures are totals over 881 tests; local timings are one input.\\n\",\"before\":325},{\"path\":\"review/rungs/wc0484-Q4-maximum-bitwise-and-after-increment-operations.md\",\"text\":\"# wc0484-Q4 Maximum Bitwise AND After Increment Operations — review note (2026-09-08)\\n\\n## Original text\\n\\n- Tempting 2: \\\"Binary search exploring mid=3 marks False and contracts hi=2; it returns 1 instead of 5\\\" on [5], k=0, m=1. Ran glm-5-cloud-t0.0's code: returns 5. With lo=0, hi=2"'^31 (or 2''^31−1) and mid=(lo+hi+1)//2 the probe sequence is exactly ans|(1<<b), b=30..0 — identical to the bit-greedy on 2000 random inputs (`probes.py`). '"\\\"Happened to pass\\\" is wrong; they are correct. The variant with hi=max(nums)+k (deepseek-flash py, glm js, kimi js: 661–667/1032) is the one that fails: [2], 0, 1 → 0, expected 2.\\n- Tempting 3: \\\"[7, 8], k = 1, m = 2\\\" is not a counterexample to top-m selection (both elements are the top 2; 8 is optimal). Re-derived: [5,3,3], k=0, m=2 (top two → 1, answer 3) and [12,5,5], k=2, m=2 (top two → 4, answer 6), by random search against the reference.\\n- Tempting 1: C(50000,2) = 1 249 975 000, fine; \\\"10x over TLE\\\" unfounded. Replaced by recorded failures: claude-haiku java cost formula target|(x&~target) (637/1032; fails [1,5], k=2, m=2 → 1, expected 2), kimi cpp lowest-missing-bit-first loop (TLE 992/1032; 2"'^b−2 iterations for x=1, mask 2''^b−1; 14.6 s locally on the harness vs ≤ 1 s for others), qwen py LIMIT=30 (1030/1032; [10''^9], 73741824, 1 → 1073741823, expected 2''^30).'"\\n- \\\"17 accepted\\\": record says 19/25.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n"'`experiments.py`: exhaustive brute force (all m-subsets, all increment distributions, k ≤ 6) vs sonnet/deepseek-pro/kimi Python on 3000 tiny inputs: 0 mismatches. `harness.py`: 3 LeetCode testcases + 50 random/edge inputs (five at n=50000, the counterexamples above), expected from deepseek-pro py.'"\\n\\n53/53: python3 claude-sonnet-4-6, deepseek-v4-pro-cloud-t0.0/t1.0, kimi, glm; java deepseek-flash, deepseek-pro, qwen, codex-agent, glm; javascript deepseek-pro; golang qwen, minimax, kimi; cpp kimi (correct, slow). Failures reproduced: deepseek-flash py 27/53, glm js 27/53, kimi js 27/53, haiku java 28/53, qwen py 47/53. Shipped code with comments: 53/53 in python3, java, javascript, golang. No accepted C++ exists; none shipped.\\n\",\"before\":319},{\"path\":\"review/rungs/wc0489-Q3-longest-almost-palindromic-substring.md\",\"text\":\"# wc0489-Q3-longest-almost-palindromic-substring — review note (2026-09-08)\\n\\n**Wrong or unverifiable in the original.**\\n- Approach 2: \\\"for "'`zzabba`, without re-expanding after the skip, the algorithm would not find ... length 5'"\\\". False: boundary-only expansion returns 5 there (core \\\"abba\\\" sits at the right edge). It fails on sample 1: "'`abca` gives 2, expected 4; shortest breaking string over a–c is `abbc`.'"\\n- Approach 1 arithmetic (3.9e13, 1.56e10) is right; \\\"~10"'^8 limit'"\\\" replaced by measurements.\\n- Approach 3 (deletion-set DP) is a strawman no submission wrote; dropped.\\n- \\\"O(n"'^2) is the target'"\\\": right exponent, but 5 of 7 Python TLEs were O(n"'^2) tables.'"\\n\\n**Verified how.** "'`gen.py`: the greedy checked equal to an O(n''^4) brute force on all binary strings ≤9 and ternary ≤6; 39 tests = 3 samples + 30 random (brute-force expected) + 6 inputs of n=2500. `tempt.py` runs four tempting variants against the brute force and reports the shortest breaking input. Timings: O(n''^3) submission 3.72 s at n=1000 (a×1000); O(n''^4) 67.7 s at n=1000 (random binary); n×n Python tables 1.0–1.3 s at n=2500 here vs LeetCode'"'s 5427 ms for the rolling variant (0.46 s here). Greedy comparisons at n=2500: 3,126,250 (a×2500), 4,688,749 ((ab)×1250).\\n\\n**Solutions tested (39/39 unless noted).** Python: qwen-t1.0 (shipped) and 8 others. Java: deepseek-flash (shipped) + 4. C++: kimi (shipped). JS: deepseek-pro (shipped) + 3. Go: minimax t1.0 (shipped), t0.0; kimi 29/39 ("'`baca` → 3, expected 4) and qwen 2/39 (`abca` → 3) match their WA verdicts.'"\\n\\n**Not settled.** The ~12× grader-vs-local Python ratio rests on one data point. "'`kimi-k2.5-cloud.py` is corrupted in the checkout (terminal escape mid-line) and cannot run.'"\\n\",\"before\":263},{\"path\":\"data/rungs/wc0484-Q4-maximum-bitwise-and-after-increment-operations.json\",\"text\":\"{\\n \\\"id\\\": \\\"wc0484-Q4-maximum-bitwise-and-after-increment-operations\\\",\\n \\\"idea_html\\\": \\\"<p>Decide the answer one bit at a time from the top. Keep <code>ans</code>; for bit b from 30 down to 0 try <code>cand = ans | (1&lt;&lt;b)</code>: for every element compute the cheapest <code>y ≥ x</code> whose bits include all of <code>cand</code> (0 if x already has them; otherwise let h be the highest bit of <code>cand</code> that x lacks, then <code>y</code> = x with bits ≤ h cleared, bit h set, and <code>cand</code>'s bits below h copied in). Sort the costs; if the m smallest sum to ≤ k, keep the bit. A set bit beats any combination of lower bits, and m elements that all contain <code>cand</code> have AND ⊇ <code>cand</code>, so the bits kept are exactly the maximum.</p><p>Example 1: nums = [3,1,2], k = 8, m = 2. Any candidate ≥ 8 costs 5, 7, 6 for the three elements; the two cheapest, 5+6 = 11 &gt; 8, so bits 30..3 stay 0. Bit 2, cand = 4: costs 1, 3, 2 (3→4, 1→4, 2→4); 1+2 = 3 ≤ 8, ans = 4. Bit 1, cand = 6: 3→6 costs 3, 1→6 costs 5, 2→6 costs 4; 3+4 = 7 ≤ 8, ans = 6. Bit 0, cand = 7: 3→7 = 4, 1→7 = 6, 2→7 = 5; 4+5 = 9 &gt; 8, rejected. Answer 6, from [6, 6] at cost 7.</p><p>31 rounds of O(n) cost computation plus a sort: O(31 · n log n) ≈ 31 · 5·10<sup>4</sup> · 16 ≈ 2.5·10<sup>7</sup> steps; O(n) extra space.</p>\\\",\\n \\\"pitfalls_html\\\": \\\"<ul><li><strong>Binary search on the answer with <code>hi = max(nums) + k</code>.</strong> feasible(T) is not monotone in T: containing the bits of 2 does not imply containing the bits of 1. On <code>nums = [2], k = 0, m = 1</code> the first probe is T = 1, which costs 1 &gt; 0, so <code>hi</code> drops to 0 and the search returns 0; the answer is 2. Three recorded Wrong Answers (661–667 of 1032) do this. The same search with <code>lo = 0, hi = 2<sup>31</sup></code> probes exactly <code>ans | (1&lt;&lt;b)</code> in order (checked on 2000 random inputs) and is the bit-by-bit method in disguise; two accepted files rely on that.</li><li><strong>Cost = <code>(target | (x &amp; ~target)) − x</code>, i.e. keep x's extra bits and set the missing ones.</strong> A carry can be cheaper. On <code>nums = [1, 5], k = 2, m = 2</code>, target 2: 5 needs only 6 (cost 1) but the formula gives 7 (cost 2), total 3 &gt; 2, so it returns 1; the answer is 2 from [2, 6]. Recorded Wrong Answer, 637/1032.</li><li><strong>Take the m largest values and optimize only those.</strong> Alignment beats size. On <code>[5, 3, 3], k = 0, m = 2</code> the top two give 5 &amp; 3 = 1, the two 3s give 3. On <code>[12, 5, 5], k = 2, m = 2</code> the top two reach at most 4; the two 5s become [6, 6] for 6.</li><li><strong>Fixing the <em>lowest</em> missing bit first, or stopping at bit 29.</strong> Each low-bit fix clears the bits below it and un-fixes them again: for x = 1 and mask 2<sup>b</sup>−1 the loop runs 2<sup>b</sup>−2 times (measured 16.7M iterations at b = 24). <code>[1073741823, 536870911], k = 0, m = 1</code> makes the recorded C++ TLE take about 14 s locally against 0.4 s for the highest-bit-first versions. Separately, values can reach 2·10<sup>9</sup> &gt; 2<sup>30</sup>: on <code>[10<sup>9</sup>], k = 73741824, m = 1</code> the answer is 2<sup>30</sup> = 1073741824, and a loop over bits 29..0 returns 1073741823 (recorded Wrong Answer, 1030/1032).</li></ul>\\\",\\n \\\"solutions\\\": {\\n  \\\"python3\\\": {\\n   \\\"code\\\": \\\"from typing import List\\\\n\\\\nclass Solution:\\\\n    def maximumAND(self, nums: List[int], k: int, m: int) -> int:\\\\n        ans = 0\\\\n        # Step 1: decide bits from the top; 31 bits are enough since values stay <= 2e9 < 2"'^31'"\\\\n        for bit in range(31, -1, -1):\\\\n            candidate = ans | (1 << bit)\\\\n            costs = []\\\\n            # Step 2: cheapest y >= a whose bits include every bit of candidate\\\\n            for a in nums:\\\\n                if (a & candidate) == candidate:\\\\n                    costs.append(0)\\\\n                else:\\\\n                    # Highest bit where candidate has 1 and a has 0\\\\n                    diff = candidate & ~a\\\\n                    i = diff.bit_length() - 1\\\\n                    # Mask to clear all bits lower than i\\\\n                    mask = ~((1 << i) - 1)\\\\n                    # Smallest number >= a with bit i set and lower bits cleared\\\\n                    y = (a & mask) + (1 << i)\\\\n                    # Ensure all bits of candidate are set\\\\n                    y |= candidate\\\\n                    costs.append(y - a)\\\\n            # Step 3: keep the bit if the m cheapest elements fit in the budget\\\\n            costs.sort()\\\\n            if sum(costs[:m]) <= k:\\\\n                ans = candidate\\\\n        return ans\\\\n\\\",\\n   \\\"model\\\": \\\"deepseek-v4-pro-cloud-t0.0\\\",\\n   \\\"runtime_ms\\\": \\\"1448 ms\\\",\\n   \\\"verified\\\": \\\"3/3 testcases + 50 random/edge (incl. 5 at n = 50000) vs reference; reference matches brute force on 3000 tiny inputs\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"java\\\": {\\n   \\\"code\\\": \\\"import java.util.Arrays;\\\\n\\\\nclass Solution {\\\\n    public int maximumAND(int[] nums, int k, int m) {\\\\n        int n = nums.length;\\\\n        int ans = 0;\\\\n        // Step 1: decide bits from the top\\\\n        for (int bit = 30; bit >= 0; bit--) {\\\\n            int candidate = ans | (1 << bit);\\\\n            long[] costs = new long[n];\\\\n            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\\\n                costs[i] = costToAchieve(nums[i], candidate);\\\\n            }\\\\n            // Step 3: keep the bit if the m cheapest elements fit in the budget\\\\n            Arrays.sort(costs);\\\\n            long sum = 0;\\\\n            for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {\\\\n                sum += costs[i];\\\\n            }\\\\n            if (sum <= k) {\\\\n                ans = candidate;\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n        return ans;\\\\n    }\\\\n\\\\n    // Step 2: cheapest y >= x whose bits include every bit of T\\\\n    private long costToAchieve(int x, int T) {\\\\n        if ((x & T) == T) return 0;\\\\n        int missing = T & ~x;\\\\n        int highest = Integer.highestOneBit(missing);   // highest bit of T that x lacks\\\\n        // set that bit, clear everything below it, then copy T's lower bits in\\\\n        long y = ((x | highest) & ~(highest - 1)) | (T & (highest - 1));\\\\n        return y - x;\\\\n    }\\\\n}\\\\n\\\",\\n   \\\"model\\\": \\\"deepseek-v4-flash-cloud-t0.0-java\\\",\\n   \\\"runtime_ms\\\": \\\"115 ms\\\",\\n   \\\"verified\\\": \\\"3/3 testcases + 50 random/edge vs reference\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"javascript\\\": {\\n   \\\"code\\\": \\\"/**\\\\n * @param {number[]} nums\\\\n * @param {number} k\\\\n * @param {number} m\\\\n * @return {number}\\\\n */\\\\nvar maximumAND = function(nums, k, m) {\\\\n    let ans = 0;\\\\n    const n = nums.length;\\\\n    const costs = new Array(n);\\\\n    // Step 1: decide bits from the top\\\\n    for (let bit = 30; bit >= 0; bit--) {\\\\n        const candidate = ans | (1 << bit);\\\\n        let zeroCount = 0;\\\\n        // Step 2: cheapest y >= a whose bits include every bit of candidate\\\\n        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\\\n            const a = nums[i];\\\\n            if ((a & candidate) === candidate) {\\\\n                costs[i] = 0;\\\\n                zeroCount++;\\\\n            } else {\\\\n                const missing = candidate & ~a;\\\\n                const h = 31 - Math.clz32(missing);           // highest missing bit\\\\n                const y = ((a >>> h) << h) | (1 << h) | candidate;   // set it, clear below, copy candidate\\\\n                costs[i] = y - a;\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n        if (zeroCount >= m) {\\\\n            ans = candidate;\\\\n            continue;\\\\n        }\\\\n        // Step 3: keep the bit if the m cheapest elements fit in the budget\\\\n        costs.sort((a, b) => a - b);\\\\n        let sum = 0;\\\\n        let possible = true;\\\\n        for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {\\\\n            sum += costs[i];\\\\n            if (sum > k) {\\\\n                possible = false;\\\\n                break;\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n        if (possible) {\\\\n            ans = candidate;\\\\n        }\\\\n    }\\\\n    return ans;\\\\n};\\\\n\\\",\\n   \\\"model\\\": \\\"deepseek-v4-pro-cloud-t0.0-javascript\\\",\\n   \\\"runtime_ms\\\": \\\"398 ms\\\",\\n   \\\"verified\\\": \\\"3/3 testcases + 50 random/edge vs reference\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"golang\\\": {\\n   \\\"code\\\": \\\"import (\\\\n\\\\t\\\\\\\"math/bits\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\t\\\\\\\"sort\\\\\\\"\\\\n)\\\\n\\\\nfunc maximumAND(nums []int, k int, m int) int {\\\\n    n := len(nums)\\\\n    costs := make([]int, n)\\\\n    ans := 0\\\\n\\\\n    // Step 1: decide bits from the top. nums[i] <= 10"'^9 < 2''^30 and k <= 10''^9,'"\\\\n    // so values stay below 2"'^31 and bit 30 is the highest that can matter.'"\\\\n    for b := 30; b >= 0; b-- {\\\\n        candidate := ans | (1 << b)\\\\n\\\\n        // Step 2: cheapest val >= num whose bits include every bit of candidate\\\\n        for i := 0; i < n; i++ {\\\\n            num := nums[i]\\\\n            if (num & candidate) == candidate {\\\\n                costs[i] = 0\\\\n            } else {\\\\n                missing := candidate & "'^num'"\\\\n                // Most significant bit that is missing (missing > 0 here)\\\\n                msb := bits.Len32(uint32(missing)) - 1\\\\n\\\\n                // high_mask keeps bits > msb from num\\\\n                high_mask := "'^(int64(1)<<(msb+1) - 1)'"\\\\n                val := int64(num) & high_mask\\\\n                // Set bit msb to 1\\\\n                val |= int64(1) << msb\\\\n                // Set required bits < msb from candidate\\\\n                val |= int64(candidate) & (int64(1)<<msb - 1)\\\\n\\\\n                costs[i] = int(val - int64(num))\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        // Step 3: keep the bit if the m cheapest elements fit in the budget\\\\n        sort.Ints(costs)\\\\n\\\\n        sum := int64(0)\\\\n        possible := true\\\\n        for i := 0; i < m; i++ {\\\\n            sum += int64(costs[i])\\\\n            if sum > int64(k) {\\\\n                possible = false\\\\n                break\\\\n            }\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        if possible {\\\\n            ans = candidate\\\\n        }\\\\n    }\\\\n\\\\n    return ans\\\\n}\\\\n\\\",\\n   \\\"model\\\": \\\"qwen3.5-cloud-t1.0-golang\\\",\\n   \\\"runtime_ms\\\": \\\"89 ms\\\",\\n   \\\"verified\\\": \\\"3/3 testcases + 50 random/edge vs reference\\\"\\n  }\\n },\\n \\\"verified_on\\\": \\\"2026-09-08\\\",\\n \\\"changes\\\": [\\n  \\\"Tempting 2 said binary search on the answer 'returns 1 instead of 5' on nums = [5], k = 0, m = 1 and that the glm files 'happened to pass'. Ran the glm code: it returns 5. With lo = 0 and hi = 2"'^31 the probes are exactly the bit-greedy candidates, so those files are correct, not lucky. The variant that really fails uses hi = max(nums)+k (three recorded WAs); replaced with [2], 0, 1.'"\\\",\\n  \\\"Tempting 3's example [7, 8], k = 1, m = 2 is not a counterexample to picking the top m values (both elements are the top 2 and 8 is optimal). Re-derived: [5,3,3], k=0, m=2 and [12,5,5], k=2, m=2, both run against the reference.\\\",\\n  \\\"Tempting 1's 'C(5*10"'^4, 2) = ~1.25*10''^9'"' is right (1 249 975 000) but '10x over TLE' is unfounded; dropped the brute-force item for four failures that were actually recorded (wrong cost formula, lowest-bit-first loop, bit-29 cutoff, non-monotone binary search).\\\",\\n  \\\"Tempting 2 counted '17 accepted solutions'; the record has 19 accepted of 25.\\\",\\n  \\\"Essence's 'rounds it up ... clears lower bits as a carry side-effect' is right; kept in the idea as the cost rule with the exact formula.\\\"\\n ]\\n}\",\"before\":251}]''')
class SimpleHTML(HTMLParser):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.stack=[]
        self.paragraphs=0
    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):
        assert tag in {'p','code','sup'} and not attrs, (tag, attrs)
        self.stack.append(tag)
        self.paragraphs += tag == 'p'
    def handle_endtag(self, tag):
        assert self.stack and self.stack.pop() == tag, tag
rows=[]
for old in originals:
    path=Path(old['path'])
    new=path.read_text()
    if path.suffix == '.json':
        before_obj=json.loads(old['text'])
        after_obj=json.loads(new)
        idea=after_obj['idea_html']
        parser=SimpleHTML()
        parser.feed(idea)
        parser.close()
        assert not parser.stack and parser.paragraphs == 3
        before_obj.pop('idea_html')
        after_obj.pop('idea_html')
        assert before_obj == after_obj, 'Other JSON keys changed'
        pattern=r'(\"idea_html\":\\s*)\"(?:["'^"'"\\\\]|\\\\.)*\"'
        assert re.sub(pattern, r'\\1\"\"', old['text']) == re.sub(pattern, r'\\1\"\"', new), 'Changes outside idea_html'
        counted=html.unescape(re.sub('<["'^>]+>'"', '', idea))
        cap=220
    else:
        for quote in re.findall(r'\"["'^"'"\\n]*\"', old['text']):
            assert quote in new, ('Missing quote', quote)
        structure=lambda s: [line for line in s.splitlines() if line.startswith('#') or line.startswith('**')]
        labels=lambda s: [re.match(r'\\*\\*.*?\\*\\*', line).group() if line.startswith('**') else line for line in structure(s)]
        assert labels(old['text']) == labels(new), 'Changed section labels'
        assert sum(line.startswith('- ') for line in old['text'].splitlines()) == sum(line.startswith('- ') for line in new.splitlines())
        counted=new
        cap=250
    count=len(counted.split())
    assert count<=cap, (path, count, cap)
    rows.append((str(path),old['before'],count))
print('| File | Before | After |')
print('| --- | ---: | ---: |')
for row in rows:
    print('| %s | %s | %s |' % row)
print('Verified caps, review structure, retained quotations, simple HTML, and JSON-only scope.')
